            "raw_output": {
              "metadata": {
                "truncated": false
              },
              "output": "```yaml\nkernelspec:\n  display_name: R 4.5.3 (xr)\n  language: R\n  name: xr\nlanguage_info:\n  codemirror_mode: ''\n  file_extension: .R\n  mimetype: text/x-R\n  name: R\n  nbconvert_exporter: ''\n  pygments_lexer: r\n  version: 4.5.3\n```\n\n### Cell 0\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-00\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n# Exercise: EDM or rap?\n\nCan a model predict an **EDM** song from a **rap** song?\n\nToday we will compare at how logistic regression and random forest models approach this question.```\n\n### Cell 1\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-01\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Setup```\n\n### Cell 2\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 1\nid: cell-02\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nlibrary('tidymodels')\n\noptions(repr.plot.res = 250, repr.plot.height = 3, repr.plot.width = 5)```\n\n### Cell 3\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-03\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## The data\n\nEach row is one song from a Spotify playlist. We keep the EDM songs and the rap songs.\n\n| column | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `track_name` | name of the song |\n| `track_artist` | who made the song |\n| `genre` | `rap` or `edm` |\n| `danceability` | how good the song is for dancing, from 0 to 1 |\n| `tempo` | speed of the beat, in beats per minute (BPM) |\n\nSource: [TidyTuesday, 2020-01-21](https://github.com/rfordatascience/tidytuesday/tree/main/data/2020/2020-01-21) (from the Spotify API, via the `spotifyr` package).\n\nSome songs are on more than one playlist. `distinct()` keeps each song one time. We put `rap` first in the factor, so `edm` is the event the model predicts.```\n\n### Cell 4\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 2\nid: cell-04\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nsongs <- read.csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rfordatascience/tidytuesday/main/data/2020/2020-01-21/spotify_songs.csv')\n\nsongs <- songs |>\n    filter(playlist_genre %in% c('edm', 'rap')) |>\n    distinct(track_id, .keep_all = TRUE) |>\n    mutate(genre = factor(playlist_genre, levels = c('rap', 'edm'))) |>\n    select(track_name, track_artist, genre, danceability, tempo)\n\nsongs |> count(genre)```\n\n### Cell 5\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-05\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 1. Look at the data\n\nPlot `tempo` (y-axis) against `danceability` (x-axis). Color the points by `genre`. There are many points, so use `alpha = 0.2` and `size = 0.5`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Hint</summary>\n\n```R\nggplot(songs, aes(x = danceability, y = tempo, color = genre)) +\n    geom_point(alpha = 0.2, size = 0.5)\n```\n\n</details>```\n\n### Cell 6\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nexecution_count: 3\nid: cell-06\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nggplot(songs, aes(x = danceability, y = tempo, color = genre)) + geom_point(alpha = 0.2, size = 0.5)```\n\n### Cell 7\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-07\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nWhere are most of the EDM songs? Where are most of the rap songs? Can you draw **one straight line** that puts the EDM songs on one side and the rap songs on the other? Can you make the legend points larger (you can ask an LLM how to do this)?```\n\n### Cell 8\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 2ae7c591-3abf-418f-af1d-e8ae7ee476b7\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\nMost of the EDM songs remain in a tempo of about 125. I could not draw one straight line that puts the EDM songs on one side and rap on the other. Generally, rap songs range a greater tempo, from about 75 to 200, but EDM songs sit in between those with an average of 125 tempo. ```\n\n### Cell 9\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-08\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 2. From a probability to a prediction\n\nFit `genre ~ danceability + tempo` with `logistic_reg()`. Save it as `fit_logit`.\n\nThen predict on `songs` with `type = 'prob'` and bind the predictions to `songs`. Save it as `preds`.\n\nMake a new column `guess` with the 0.5 rule: `'edm'` if `.pred_edm` is more than 0.5, `'rap'` if not. Then find the accuracy: the mean of `guess == genre`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Hint</summary>\n\n```R\nfit_logit <- logistic_reg() |> fit(genre ~ danceability + tempo, data = songs)\n\npreds <- fit_logit |>\n    predict(new_data = songs, type = 'prob') |>\n    bind_cols(songs) |>\n    mutate(guess = if_else(.pred_edm > 0.5, 'edm', 'rap'))\n\npreds |> summarize(accuracy = mean(guess == genre))\n```\n\n</details>```\n\n### Cell 10\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-09\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\n```\n\n### Cell 11\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-10\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n**Check:** the accuracy should be about 0.60.\n\nAbout half of the songs are EDM. So a model that just predicts all songs are EDM is right about 0.50 of the time. Is 0.60 good?\n\nBonus check: predict with `type = 'class'` and compare `.pred_class` with your `guess` column. They should be the same.```\n\n### Cell 12\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-11\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\n```\n\n### Cell 13\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-12\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 3. The logistic regression boundary\n\nMake a grid:\n\n```R\ngrid <- crossing(danceability = seq(0.2, 1, by = 0.01), tempo = seq(60, 210, by = 1))\n```\n\nCopy the `plot_boundary()` function from the lecture into the cell below. Change it for this data:\n\n- `x = danceability`, `y = tempo`\n- `.pred_edm` instead of `.pred_yes`\n\nThen run `plot_boundary(fit_logit, grid)`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Hint</summary>\n\n```R\ngrid <- crossing(danceability = seq(0.2, 1, by = 0.01), tempo = seq(60, 210, by = 1))\n\nplot_boundary <- function(fit, grid) {\n    preds <- fit |>\n        predict(new_data = grid, type = 'prob') |>\n        bind_cols(grid)\n\n    ggplot(preds, aes(x = danceability, y = tempo)) +\n        geom_tile(aes(fill = .pred_edm)) +\n        geom_contour(aes(z = .pred_edm), breaks = 0.5, color = 'black') +\n        scale_fill_gradient2(low = 'steelblue', mid = 'white', high = 'darkorange',\n                             midpoint = 0.5, limits = c(0, 1))\n}\n\nplot_boundary(fit_logit, grid)\n```\n\n</details>```\n\n### Cell 14\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-13\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\n```\n\n### Cell 15\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-14\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n**Check:** you should see one straight black line. Low danceability is orange (guess EDM). High danceability is blue (guess rap).\n\nCompare with your plot in step 1. What does this line miss?```\n\n### Cell 16\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-15\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 4. AI step: a random forest\n\nOpen a terminal and run `opencode`.```\n\n### Cell 17\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-16\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n**Copy this prompt into opencode:**```\n\n### Cell 18\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-17\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n```text\nI am working in exercises/13-spotify-exercise.ipynb, with R and tidymodels.\nThe data frame songs has a factor column genre (rap or edm) and two number\ncolumns, danceability and tempo.\n\nIn the empty code cell in step 4, fit a random forest of\ngenre ~ danceability + tempo with rand_forest(trees = 200),\nset_mode('classification'), and set_engine('ranger'). Call set.seed(512)\nfirst. Save it as fit_rf. Run the cell. Use tidymodels only. Do not change\nany other cells.\n``````\n\n### Cell 19\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-18\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n**Your assistant puts its code in the cell below and runs it.** Read the code. Does it use `rand_forest()`? Does it call `set.seed()` before the fit?```\n\n### Cell 20\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-19\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\n```\n\n### Cell 21\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-20\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 5. The random forest boundary\n\nRun `plot_boundary(fit_rf, grid)`.\n\nThen find the accuracy of `fit_rf` on `songs`. Use `type = 'class'` this time.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Hint</summary>\n\n```R\nplot_boundary(fit_rf, grid)\n\nfit_rf |>\n    predict(new_data = songs, type = 'class') |>\n    bind_cols(songs) |>\n    summarize(accuracy = mean(.pred_class == genre))\n```\n\n</details>```\n\n### Cell 22\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-21\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\n```\n\n### Cell 23\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: cell-22\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n**Check:** you should see an orange **stripe** across the plot, near 125 BPM. The accuracy should be about 0.94.\n\n- Look at the step 1 plot again. Why is there a stripe? (Hint: many EDM songs have a beat of about 125 to 130 BPM.)\n- Why can't logistic regression draw a stripe?\n- The lecture said to be careful with accuracy on the same songs we used to fit the model. Look for small islands in your plot. Do you think 0.94 is too high?```"
