    {
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1793639496.369565,
      "body": "The step 3 chart is a **decision-boundary heatmap** for the logistic regression model.\n\n**How to read it:**\n- **x-axis** = `danceability`, **y-axis** = `tempo` \u2014 a full grid of every combination (`crossing(...)`).\n- **Fill color** = the model's predicted probability that a song is **EDM** (`.pred_edm`). Orange = high probability of EDM, blue = low probability (i.e., predicts rap), white = 50/50.\n- **Black line** = the 0.5 contour: the exact spot where the model switches its guess from rap to edm.\n\n**What it tells you:**\n- The boundary is a **single straight line** because logistic regression is a linear model \u2014 the set of points where the log-odds equal zero is always a straight line. It cannot bend.\n- The color bands run mostly **vertically**, meaning `danceability` does almost all the work: low danceability \u2192 orange (guessed EDM), high danceability \u2192 blue (guessed rap). `tempo` barely shifts the prediction (just tilts the line slightly).\n- This is the key limitation the exercise is pointing at: the real EDM songs cluster in a **horizontal stripe near 125 BPM** (as you saw in step 1), but one straight line can't isolate a stripe. It has to split the plane in half, which is why logistic regression only reaches ~0.60 accuracy.\n\nIn short: the chart shows the model's \"best straight-line guess,\" and visually demonstrates *why* a linear boundary is a poor fit for this data.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::OpenCodeAcpPersona",
